# s32: the hillside vegetation (grass/bush/fern/leaf cards) comes out as soft scribbled lines.
# These are the alpha-cutout card textures of the game's procedural-vegetation objects: every
# texture in the dictionaries those objects reference, kept only where the texture really has
# cutout content (>=5 % fully transparent or >=5 % mid-alpha texels; the opaque bark/dirt in the
# same dictionaries is left alone). Regenerate with:
#   python3 tools/vegetation_hashes.py models/gta3.img --emit rtx.hairCardTextures
# (the dictionary allow-list is the tool's default; --verbose prints the per-texture alpha table).
# Measured on the mip-0 alpha: gta_proc_rushes gras03Si 76.5 % alpha 0 / 20.8 % mid / 2.7 %
# opaque; gta_proc_grassland oak2b 89.8 / 10.2 / 0; gta_proc_bush newtreeleaves128 30.2 / 20.6 /
# 49.2. The cards are painted with soft gradients and not one of them is in any rtx.conf category
# list today, so the runtime blends them instead of alpha-testing them - which is what the
# hair-card tooltip is for ("Textures in this category will preserve fine texture detail, render
# as cutouts instead of alpha blends, and disable backface culling"), and it is also what makes
# the opacity micromaps apply to them ("a ground densely covered with grass blades ... multiple
# trees with a lot of foliage").
# If the vegetation looks unchanged in game, this line may be too long for the parser: retry with
# the procobj-only subset (45 hashes, 922 chars) - .strata/notes-s32-water-vegetation.md.
